            "level": {
                "value": 9
            },
            "privateNotes": "",
            "publicNotes": "<p>These fey creatures appear as road-worn travelers with a coin purse and flask at their belt, carrying a staff bearing a turnip with a glowing, grinning face. When wandering the road at night, should you see a figure holding up a lantern in the distance, take care—it could be a jack-o'-lantern.</p>\n<p>Legends say the original Jack of the Lantern was a greedy trickster who fooled a beggar out of his last silver coin. To punish him, an archon cursed the coin so that any who held it would be rooted in place until released by the coin's owner. Luckily for Jack, he already had the coin in his purse and avoided the curse.</p>\n<p>When a powerful devil later tried to bargain for Jack's soul, Jack left the shining coin on the table. The greedy devil couldn't resist it and was rooted to the ground for years, causing a bloody revolution in the devil's holdings in Hell.</p>\n<p>When Jack died, Heaven refused him on principle, and Hell refused him out of embarrassment, leaving his restless soul to wander. Amused at Jack's antics, the Lantern King placed a speck of his own essence into a carved turnip, offering the implement of power to Jack in return for his eternal service. Ever since, echoes of Jack—jack-o'-lanterns—have roamed the planes at the Lantern King's command, spreading chaos and trickery.</p>",
            "publication": {
                "license": "ORC",
                "remaster": true,
                "title": "Pathfinder Feybound"
            }
        },
